双胍格列本脲片Ⅱ (metformin-glibenclamide tablets II), increasing the risk of lactic acidosis and unpredictable glycemic control. Metformin-containing products are associated with 乳酸酸中毒 (lactic acidosis), a rare but life-threatening condition exacerbated by conditions that impair lactate clearance—such as alcohol-induced hepatic dysfunction. Additionally, alcohol may alter the metabolism of sulfonylureas, leading to either reduced efficacy or enhanced hypoglycemia [Data: Entities (3488, 11518); Relationships (8509, 51960)]. This dual risk necessitates careful monitoring and patient education.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Cardiovascular drugs show additive hypotensive effects with alcohol\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Several cardiovascular agents, including 硝酸甘油气雾剂 (nitroglycerin aerosol) and 复方地巴唑氢氯噻嗪 (compound dibazol-hydrochlorothiazide), demonstrate clinically relevant interactions with alcohol that result in enhanced hypotension. Nitroglycerin use during moderate or heavy drinking can cause significant drops in blood pressure, increasing fall and syncope risk. Similarly, hydrochlorothiazide—a component of 复方地巴唑氢氯噻嗪—interacts with 硫酸胍生 (guanethidine sulfate), another antihypertensive, and both are susceptible to alcohol-induced potentiation of orthostatic hypotension [Data: Entities (7404, 4690, 7461); Relationships (46535, 34589)]. These interactions are especially concerning in elderly populations.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Disulfiram itself causes adverse reactions mimicking alcohol intolerance\",\n            \"explanation\": \"双硫醒 (disulfiram), used as an aversive agent in alcohol dependence treatment, independently induces adverse effects such as 低血压 (hypotension), nausea, vomiting, and facial flushing when alcohol is consumed. This reaction stems from disulfiram’s irreversible inhibition of aldehyde dehydrogenase, causing toxic acetaldehyde buildup. The severity can be life-threatening, reinforcing why disulfiram-alcohol co-exposure is a medical emergency [Data: Entities (4175); Relationships (9853)]. This mechanism also explains why other drugs with similar enzyme-inhibiting properties (e.g., certain cephalosporins) produce identical clinical syndromes.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Alcohol does not significantly affect olanzapine blood concentration\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Despite extensive interactions with other psychotropic drugs, alcohol does not meaningfully alter 奥氮平血浓度 (olanzapine blood concentration). This is because olanzapine is metabolized by CYP1A2, 2D6, 3A4, 2C19, and 2C9 enzymes, but not by CYP2E1—the primary enzyme induced by alcohol. Therefore, unlike many other CNS depressants, olanzapine’s pharmacokinetics remain stable in the presence of alcohol [Data: Entities (13472); Relationships (4498)]. This represents a notable exception in the otherwise high-risk landscape of alcohol-drug interactions.\"\n        }\n    ],\n    \"rating\": 7.5,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The community poses a high clinical impact due to the prevalence of alcohol use and the severe, sometimes fatal, adverse reactions resulting from its interactions with commonly prescribed medications.\"\n}", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": null, "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null, "provider_specific_fields": {"refusal": null}}, "provider_specific_fields": {}}], "created": 1782121871, "model": "qwen3-max", "object": "chat.completion", "moderation": null, "service_tier": null, "system_fingerprint": null, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1685, "prompt_tokens": 6041, "total_tokens": 7726, "completion_tokens_details": null, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 1280}}, "formatted_response": null, "content": "{\n    \"title\": \"Alcohol-Drug Interaction Network in Clinical Pharmacology\",\n    \"summary\": \"This community centers on alcohol (乙醇) and its clinically significant interactions wi